@page "/learn/walkthrough"
@rendermode InteractiveServer

<PageTitle>The Guided Tour · Sieve School</PageTitle>

<header class="page-head">
    <SectionIcon Name="tour" />
    <div>
        <h1>The guided tour</h1>
        <p>A worked quadratic-sieve factorization of 15347 = 103 × 149.</p>
    </div>
</header>

<section class="panel walkthrough" tabindex="0" @onkeydown="KeyStep">
    <p class="walkthrough__kicker">@Slides[_index].Kicker</p>
    <h2>@Slides[_index].Title</h2>
    @switch (_index)
    {
        case 0:
            <p>Multiplying 103 × 149 is instant; recovering 103 and 149 from 15347 is the hard direction. RSA relies on
                exactly this asymmetry.</p>
                <p class="formula">103 × 149 → 15347</p>
                break;
        case 1:

                    <p>If X<sup>2</sup> ≡ Y<sup>2</sup> (mod N) and X ≢ ±Y, then gcd(X − Y, N) is a nontrivial factor.
                        Everything that follows finds such a pair.</p>
                        <p class="formula">X<sup>2</sup> − Y<sup>2</sup> = (X − Y)(X + Y)</p>
                        break;
        case 2:

                            <p>Collect many relations x<sup>2</sup> ≡ small product (mod N), then multiply a subset whose prime
                                exponents are all even. That product is a square.</p>
                                break;
        case 3:

                                    <p>For N = 15347 and bound 30, the usable primes are {2, 17, 23, 29}. A larger base finds smooth values more
                                        easily, but grows the later matrix.</p>
                                        break;
        case 4:

                                            <p>Use y(x) = (x + 124)<sup>2</sup> − 15347. Add log p at the positions where each prime divides y(x), just
                                                like Eratosthenes.</p>
                                                <SieveDemo />
                                                break;
        case 5:

                                                    <table>
                                                        <thead>
                                                            <tr>
                                                                <th>x</th>
                                                                <th>x + 124</th>
                                                                <th>y(x)</th>
                                                                <th>factorization</th>
                                                            </tr>
                                                        </thead>
                                                        <tbody>
                                                            <tr>
                                                                <td>0</td>
                                                                <td>124</td>
                                                                <td>29</td>
                                                                <td>29</td>
                                                            </tr>
                                                            <tr>
                                                                <td>3</td>
                                                                <td>127</td>
                                                                <td>782</td>
                                                                <td>2 · 17 · 23</td>
                                                            </tr>
                                                            <tr>
                                                                <td>71</td>
                                                                <td>195</td>
                                                                <td>22678</td>
                                                                <td>2 · 17 · 23 · 29</td>
                                                            </tr>
                                                        </tbody>
                                                    </table>
                                                    <p>Real runs also retain partial relations with one large leftover prime, then combine pairs later.</p>
                                                    break;
                                                case 6:

                                                    <p>Filtering removes singletons, merges duplicates, and combines partials. The result is a smaller matrix
                                                        where every remaining row has a chance to pair into a square.</p>
                                                        break;
        case 7:

                                                            <p>Write prime exponents modulo 2: v₁ = (0,0,0,1), v₂ = (1,1,1,0), v₃ = (1,1,1,1). Here v₁ + v₂ + v₃ = 0, so
                                                                all three relations make a square.</p>
                                                                break;
        case 8:

                                                                    <p>X = 124 · 127 · 195 mod 15347 = 1460. Y = 2 · 17 · 23 · 29 ≡ 7331 (mod 15347). gcd(15347, 7331 − 1460) =
                                                                        gcd(15347, 5871) = 103.</p>
                                                                        <p class="formula">15347 / 103 = 149</p>
                                                                        break;
                                                                        default:

<p>That is the quadratic sieve: choose a factor base, sieve for relations, filter, solve parity equations,
                                                                            and take a gcd. The pipeline turns this compact story into a practical factorization.</p>
                                                                            <p><a href="/history">Where did all this come from? → History</a></p>
                                                                            break;
                                                                        }
                                                                        <div class="walkthrough__controls"><button type="button" @onclick="Back" disabled="@(_index == 0)">Back</button>
                                                                            <div class="walkthrough__dots">@for (var index = 0; index < Slides.Length; index++) { var dot = index;
                                                                                <button
                                                                                type="button" class="@(dot == _index ? "active" : string.Empty)" @onclick="() => _index = dot"
                                                                                aria-label="@($"Go to step {dot + 1}")"></button>
                                                                            }
                                                                        </div><button class="primary" type="button"
                                                                                      @onclick="Next" disabled="@(_index == Slides.Length - 1)">Next</button>
                                                                    </div>
                                                                </section>

@code {
    private static readonly WalkthroughSlide[] Slides =
    [
        new("Why factoring is hard", "The problem"), new("Fermat's old trick", "The key identity"), new("Make squares from pieces", "Kraitchik's idea"), new("The factor base", "Pipeline stage 1"), new("Sieving", "Pipeline stage 2"),
new("Relations", "The harvest"), new("Filtering", "Pipeline stage 3"), new("Linear algebra", "Pipeline stage 4"),
new("Square root", "Pipeline stage 5"), new("The whole sieve", "Put it together"),
    ];
    private int _index;
    private void Back() => _index--;
    private void Next() => _index++;
    private void KeyStep(KeyboardEventArgs args)
    {
        if (args.Key == "ArrowLeft" && _index > 0) { Back(); }
        else if (args.Key
== "ArrowRight" && _index < Slides.Length - 1) { Next(); }
    }
    private sealed record WalkthroughSlide(string Title, string Kicker);
}
